{{/*
The pull-request workflow: the declared checks (github_ci.checks), and nothing
else.

Only generated for a project that declares checks — see GetMainTemplates, which
drops this file from the output set otherwise, so a project without checks gets
no workflow with an empty matrix in it.

Why a workflow of its own rather than a pull_request trigger on ci_cd.yml:
every job of that workflow hangs off prepare-env, which resolves the build
variables of a *branch* (ENABLED_<BRANCH>, the deployment hosts, the port
prefixes). On a pull request GITHUB_REF is refs/pull/<N>/merge, so there is no
such branch and no such variable; the checks job would report `skipped` while
the run stayed green, and the build and deployment jobs would have to be shut
off one `if:` at a time — each one silent when it is wrong. Here there is
nothing to shut off: the file has one job, and the job is the checks.

The branch filters are the ones of the push trigger (github_ci.push_branches):
they are the project's statement of which branches its CI covers, and a pull
request into such a branch is a push into it with a review in between.
*/ -}}
name: PR Checks

on:
  pull_request:
    branches:
      {{ $.GithubCI.PushBranchesBlock }}

jobs:
{{- template "checksJob" $.ChecksOnPullRequest }}
